---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The comprehensive Mustache reference — the front door (debate consensus item
// 8). Prose is illustrative only; every runnable example comes from the single
// gate-validated source `../mustache.mjs`; the one feature MinBars cannot execute
// (lambdas) is a static `spec-only` callout with no live button.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import SurfaceDeltas from "../components/SurfaceDeltas.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../mustache.mjs";
// The one spec-only block (lambdas) can't run, so it's static — but still
// syntax-highlighted at build time through the same highlighter the live editors
// use, so it reads consistently.
import { highlightTemplate } from "../lib/highlight.mjs";
// Conformance numbers are GENERATED from the vendored mustache/spec suite
// (scripts/gen-conformance.mjs, gated by `npm run check:conformance`), so the
// table below can never claim more than the suite proves on the shipped engine.
import conformance from "../conformance.json";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/spec/";
const conformanceGreen = conformance.modules.every((m) => m.pass === m.total);

// Sticky left-nav model (consensus item 1): Mustache concepts, then the sibling
// surfaces this reference is the hub for, then the Lab.
const groups = [
  {
    label: "Mustache",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What Mustache is" },
      { href: "#reference", label: "Surface reference" },
      { href: "#interpolation", label: "Interpolation" },
      { href: "#sections", label: "Sections" },
      { href: "#truthiness", label: "Truthiness (gotcha)" },
      { href: "#inverted", label: "Inverted sections" },
      { href: "#comments", label: "Comments" },
      { href: "#partials", label: "Partials" },
      { href: "#dynamic-names", label: "Dynamic names" },
      { href: "#set-delimiters", label: "Set delimiters" },
      { href: "#lambdas", label: "Lambdas" },
      { href: "#conformance", label: "What MinBars supports" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "New here?",
    items: [{ href: "/start", label: "Getting started" }],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/rawbars", label: "RawBars — the core" },
      { href: "/fullbars", label: "FullBars — Handlebars" },
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars — operators" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=minbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references and spec-only code — kept in strings so Astro never
// tries to parse the `{{ }}` as expressions.
const t = (s) => s; // readability helper for inline tag literals
// Lambdas are the one Mustache feature MinBars does not implement (no function
// arm in `Value`), so this stays a static, syntax-highlighted spec-only block.
const lambdaCode = `// mustache.js — a section whose value is a function (a "lambda")
const data = {
  year: 2026,
  bold: () => (text, render) => "<b>" + render(text) + "</b>",
};
Mustache.render("{{#bold}}(c) {{year}}{{/bold}}", data);
// → "<b>(c) 2026</b>"`;
const lambdaHtml = highlightTemplate(lambdaCode, "minbars");

// Worked example (P5): a *value* lambda → a precalculated plain-data field —
// shown as static code (what you'd write as a lambda, and the JSONata that
// precomputes it). No live example: with the value precomputed there is no
// lambda left in the data to see, which only reads as confusing.
const valueLambdaCode = `// Mustache / Handlebars.js — a value lambda (a function on the data)
const data = {
  first: "Ada", last: "Lovelace",
  fullName() { return this.first + " " + this.last; },
};
// template:  {{fullName}}   →   "Ada Lovelace"`;
const jsonataCode = `/* JSONata transform — compute the value, emit plain data */
$ ~> | $ | {
  "fullName": first & " " & last,
  "initials": $uppercase($substring(first, 0, 1) & $substring(last, 0, 1))
} |`;
---

<Reference
  title="MinBars — Mustache-compatible"
  subtitle="MinBars is FlatBars's Mustache-compatible engine. This runnable reference walks the logic-less Mustache language as MinBars implements it — every supported tag renders live and opens in the Lab."
  groups={groups}
  spec="adr-0007-minbars"
  dialect="minbars"
>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What Mustache is</h2>
    <p>
      Mustache is <b>logic-less</b>: there are no <code>if</code> statements, no
      <code>else</code> clauses, no loops. A template is plain text with
      <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code> tags; rendering expands each tag against the data
      you hand it. All control flow is expressed by the <em>shape of the data</em>
      (is this key truthy? is it a list?), never by code in the template.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mustache is also <b>a specification, not an engine</b> — like how JavaScript
      is a spec that Chrome and Firefox implement. Dozens of engines across
      languages implement it. The engine here is <b>MinBars</b>, the
      Mustache-compatible member of the FlatBars family: it passes the vendored
      <code>mustache/spec</code> suite for every feature on this page (lambdas
      excepted — see <a href="#conformance">what MinBars supports</a>).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.hello.template} data={ex.hello.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The panes are live: edit the <b>template</b> or the <b>data</b> and the
      output re-renders as you type. When you want the full editor,
      <b>Open in Lab</b> carries the same example over.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Surface reference ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="reference">
    <h2>Surface reference</h2>
    <p>The syntax and value-policy deltas that define MinBars (Mustache), at a glance — generated
      from the same single source (<code>shared/surface-syntax.json</code>) the FlatBars Lab's
      Reference popup and the spec engine page read, so it can never drift from what they render.</p>
    <SurfaceDeltas surface="minbars" />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Interpolation ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="interpolation">
    <h2>Interpolation</h2>
    <p>
      The workhorse tag. <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> looks <code>name</code> up
      in the data and inserts it, <b>HTML-escaped</b> by default (so user data
      can't inject markup). To emit raw, unescaped output use the triple-mustache
      <code>{t("{{{name}}}")}</code> or the ampersand form <code>{t("{{&name}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.escaping.template} data={ex.escaping.data} />

    <h3>Names are just keys</h3>
    <p>
      Mustache has no identifier grammar. A name is the tag's text, trimmed, looked
      up as a literal key in your data — so almost anything works:
      <code>{t("{{first-name}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{active?}}")}</code>, even
      <code>{t("{{2nd}}")}</code>. The only characters a bare name can't contain are
      whitespace, <code>.</code> (the segment separator, below) and the active
      delimiters <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code>. Nesting is the one bit of structure: a
      dot splits the lookup into a path.
    </p>

    <h3>Dotted names</h3>
    <p>Reach into nested objects with dots: <code>{t("{{name.first}}")}</code>.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.dotted.template} data={ex.dotted.data} />

    <h3>Missing keys</h3>
    <p>
      A name that resolves to nothing — missing or <code>null</code> — renders as
      the empty string. Mustache never errors on a missing key.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.missing.template} data={ex.missing.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Sections ────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="sections">
    <h2>Sections</h2>
    <p>
      A section <code>{t("{{#key}} … {{/key}}")}</code> renders its body a number
      of times that depends on the <em>shape</em> of <code>key</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>List</b> → the body repeats once per element, each element pushed as the context.</li>
      <li><b>Truthy non-list</b> (object, <code>true</code>, a number, a string) → the body renders once.</li>
      <li><b>Falsy</b> (<code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>[]</code>) → the body is skipped.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Iterating a list</h3>
    <p>
      Over a list of scalars, <code>{t("{{.}}")}</code> — the <em>implicit
      iterator</em> — is the current item itself.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.sectionArray.template} data={ex.sectionArray.data} />

    <h3>An object opens a scope</h3>
    <p>When the value is an object, that object becomes the context for the body.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.sectionObject.template} data={ex.sectionObject.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Truthiness ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="truthiness">
    <h2>Truthiness — the one that bites</h2>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <p>
        <b>In Mustache, only <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, and the empty
        list <code>[]</code> are falsy.</b> The number <code>0</code> and the
        empty string <code>""</code> are <b>truthy</b> — so a section over them
        <em>renders</em>. This is the single most common surprise for anyone
        arriving from Handlebars or JavaScript, where <code>0</code> and
        <code>""</code> are falsy.
      </p>
      <p>
        And this is <em>why there is no <code>if</code></em>: a section like
        <code>{t("{{#count}}")}</code> is not a conditional, it is a section
        <em>keyed on the shape</em> of <code>count</code> — it renders when the
        value is truthy and hides otherwise. No <code>else if</code>, no helper;
        a missing key simply hides its block.
      </p>
    </div>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>value</th><th>Mustache / MinBars</th><th>JS / Handlebars</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>false</code></td><td>falsy</td><td>falsy</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>null</code></td><td>falsy</td><td>falsy</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>[]</code></td><td>falsy</td><td>falsy / truthy*</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td><b>truthy</b></td><td>falsy</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>""</code></td><td><b>truthy</b></td><td>falsy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>See it for yourself — <code>count</code> is <code>0</code> yet the section renders:</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.truthyZero.template} data={ex.truthyZero.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────── Inverted sections ────────────────────── -->
  <section id="inverted">
    <h2>Inverted sections</h2>
    <p>
      Open with a caret — <code>{t("{{^key}} … {{/key}}")}</code> — to render the
      body <em>only</em> when the key is falsy. The idiomatic "list, or an
      empty-state message" pairs a section with its inverse.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.inverted.template} data={ex.inverted.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Comments ────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="comments">
    <h2>Comments</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code> is dropped entirely from the output — handy for
      notes that should never reach the rendered page.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.comment.template} data={ex.comment.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Partials ────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="partials">
    <h2>Partials</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{> name}}")}</code> includes another template by name. A partial
      <b>inherits the caller's context</b> — it sees the same data — which makes
      partials the natural unit for reuse: a row, a card, a header/footer.
    </p>
    <h3>A partial per row</h3>
    <p>
      Combine a section with a partial to template each element of a list. Note
      where the line break lives: a standalone <code>{t("{{> row}}")}</code> line
      has its own newline <b>trimmed</b>, so the partial body owns its trailing
      newline — otherwise every row would run together on one line.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.partialList.template} data={ex.partialList.data} partials={ex.partialList.partials} />
    <p class="tip">
      Partials also carry <b>standalone indentation</b>: if a <code>{t("{{> p}}")}</code>
      sits alone on an indented line, that indent is re-applied to every line the
      partial emits — so included blocks stay aligned.
    </p>

    <h3>Composing HTML with partials</h3>
    <p>
      When your output <em>is</em> HTML, partials compose it: a
      <code>{t("{{> styles}}")}</code> partial holds the CSS once and a
      <code>{t("{{> card}}")}</code> partial is the markup for one row, while the
      section just iterates. Mustache stays logic-less — the partials carry the
      structure and the style. (This is the one example that previews as HTML in
      the Lab rather than plain text.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.card.template} data={ex.card.data} partials={ex.card.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Dynamic names ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="dynamic-names">
    <h2>Dynamic names</h2>
    <p>
      Choose the partial (or parent) <em>at render time</em> by prefixing a star
      and reading the name from the data: <code>{t("{{>* key}}")}</code> includes
      whichever partial <code>key</code> resolves to.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.dynamicPartial.template} data={ex.dynamicPartial.data} partials={ex.dynamicPartial.partials} />

    <h3>Putting it together</h3>
    <p>
      Real templates reuse these familiar pieces at once. A plain-text shipping
      notice: interpolation, a dotted path, an inverted fallback, and a list
      section that renders each row through a <code>{t("{{> item}}")}</code>
      partial — no new concepts, just combined.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.email.template} data={ex.email.data} partials={ex.email.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Set delimiters ────────────────────── -->
  <section id="set-delimiters">
    <h2>Set delimiters</h2>
    <p>
      A template can <em>change its own delimiters</em> mid-stream with
      <code>{t("{{=<% %>=}}")}</code> — useful when <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code>
      collides with another syntax (e.g. server-side languages). Everything after
      the tag uses the new pair until <code>{t("<%={{ }}=%>")}</code> restores the
      default. The delimiters may be any non-whitespace strings (no <code>=</code>),
      and a set-delimiter tag alone on its line is <b>standalone</b> — it leaves no
      blank line. Edit the example: the middle item switches to ERB-style tags.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={ex.setDelimiters.template} data={ex.setDelimiters.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── Lambdas ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="lambdas">
    <h2>Lambdas <span class="badge spec-only">precalculated in FlatBars</span></h2>
    <p>
      The spec allows a key to be a <em>function</em>. A lambda used as a section
      receives the raw, unrendered body text and a <code>render</code> callback,
      so it can transform or wrap content programmatically.
    </p>
    <pre class="oil-static"><code set:html={lambdaHtml} /></pre>
    <div class="callout info">
      <p>
        FlatBars keeps <b><code>Value</code> pure data — never a function</b>, so a lambda lives
        outside the value. Its two jobs split cleanly: a <b>value-producing lambda</b> (format a
        date, compute a total) is <b>precalculated before rendering</b> — in the Lab, with the
        <a href="/transform">JSONata transform</a> (first-class functions and
        closures), though any host-side computation works; the engine just receives plain data. A
        <b>section lambda</b> that rewrites its block body is a <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a>
        <b>helper</b>, where render-time behaviour belongs — a preprocess step never sees the
        unrendered body. The guarantee: <em>no arbitrary host code runs from template data, and the
        rendered value stays JSON-serialisable.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked example: precompute the value</h3>
    <p>
      Say you want a <code>fullName</code> from <code>first</code> + <code>last</code>. In Mustache
      you'd put a <em>function</em> on the data:
    </p>
    <pre class="oil-static"><code>{valueLambdaCode}</code></pre>
    <p>
      In FlatBars you compute it <b>before</b> rendering — here with a
      <a href="/transform">JSONata transform</a> (its first-class functions and closures do the
      work) — and emit plain data:
    </p>
    <pre class="oil-static"><code>{jsonataCode}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The renderer then sees ordinary fields, so <code>{"{{fullName}}"}</code> is a plain
      interpolation — identical interpreted and compiled, with no function in the context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Conformance ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conformance">
    <h2>What MinBars supports</h2>
    <p>
      Every row below is <b>measured, not asserted</b>: the counts come from
      running the vendored <code>mustache/spec</code> suite through the exact
      engine these live examples use, regenerated on every build. The page can't
      claim a pass its own previews would contradict.
    </p>
    <p class="score">
      {conformanceGreen ? "✓ " : ""}MinBars passes
      <b>{conformance.total.pass}/{conformance.total.total}</b>
      of the vendored mustache/spec suite.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Spec cases passed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {conformance.modules.map((m) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{m.label}</td>
            <td class={m.pass === m.total && m.total > 0 ? "ok" : "no"}>
              {m.pass === m.total && m.total > 0 ? "✓ " : ""}{m.pass}/{m.total}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
        <tr><td>Lambdas</td><td>→ <a href="#lambdas">out of the value by design</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      The table above is the core suite. Measured against the <b>full official
      <code>mustache/spec</code></b> — the optional <code>dynamic-names</code> and
      <code>inheritance</code> modules included — MinBars passes <b>100% (184/184) of every module
      it implements</b>. The optional <code>lambdas</code> module isn't a target: a lambda is a
      <em>function in the data</em>, and a <code>Value</code> is pure data
      (<a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0007-minbars/`}>ADR-0007</a>) — value-producing lambdas are
      <a href="#lambdas">precalculated before rendering</a>, section lambdas are helpers. The proof
      lives in <code>conformance/mustache/</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Where to go next</h3>
    <p>
      Mustache is the on-ramp. When you want real conditionals, helpers, and
      computed values, the FlatBars ladder continues:
      <a href="/fullbars">FullBars</a> adds Handlebars-style blocks and helpers,
      and <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> adds infix operators and pipes — all on
      the same core. Or jump straight into the
      <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=minbars">Lab</a> and start typing.
    </p>
  </section>

</Reference>
